   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the local finance law, in relation to real property tax refunds and credits for participation in the Lake Ontario and connected waterways assessment relief act; and to amend chapter 54 of the laws of 2017, enacting the Capital Projects Budget, in relation to the services and expenses of the Lake Ontario and connected waterways assessment relief act",
    "basePrintNo": "S6514",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6514-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 62,
       "fullName": "Robert Ortt",
       "imgName": "885_robert_ortt.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 885,
       "sessionMemberId": 957,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "ORTT"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1, Chap 54 of 2017; amd §11.00, Loc Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Local Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6514\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the local finance law, in relation\nto real property tax refunds and credits for participation in the Lake\nOntario and connected waterways assessment relief act; and to amend\nchapter 54 of the laws of 2017, enacting the Capital Projects Budget,\nin relation to the services and expenses of the Lake Ontario and\nconnected waterways assessment relief act\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo provide property tax relief for those affected by the flooding and\nrising water levels in the areas surrounding Lake Ontario; and to\ncreate a fund to which eligible municipalities may apply for monies to\nhelp cover any resulting losses of tax revenues.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Title of the act - \"Lake Ontario and Connected Waterways\nAssessment Relief Act\"\n\nSection 2. Definition of terms used in this act.\n\nSection 3. Provides guidelines and timeframes relevant to any eligible\nmunicipality adopting the provisions of the act.\n\nSection 4. Outlines the amount a property assessment can be reduced\ndue to damage caused by flooding; as well as detailing the process by\nwhich an individual may apply for such relief to the local assessor,\nthe process by which an assessor shall issue such relief, and how an\nindividual can challenge such determinations made by the assessor.\n\nSection 5. The commissioner of tax and finance is authorized to\ndevelop guidance to be used by local assessing units for\nimplementation of this act.\n\nSection 6. Provides that school districts be held harmless by the\nState for any reduction in State Aid that would have been paid as a\ntax savings pursuant to § 1306-a of the RPTL.\n\nSection 7. Establishes the Lake Ontario and Connected Waterways Flood\nMitigation Program to which eligible municipalities can apply for\nfunds to help cover the cost of lost tax revenues due to flooding and\nrising lake levels.\n\nSection 8. Specifies the amounts to be contributed to the urban\ndevelopment corporation in order to effectuate this act in FY 2017-18\nand FY 2018-19.\n\nSection 9. Appropriates funds in the amount necessary to fund the Lake\nOntario and Connected Waterways Flood Mitigation Program.\n\nSections 10 and 11. Provides local municipalities the authority to\nbond in relation to this act. Section 12. Severability clause.\n\nSection 13. Effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nSince April 12, 2017, Lake Ontario has risen 18 inches causing record\nhigh lake levels, widespread flooding, and property damage across\nlakeshore communities in New York state. According to the U.S. Army\nCorps of Engineers, over the past six weeks, nearly 4 trillion gallons\nof rain water has flowed into Lake Ontario. Unfortunately, for\nlakeside communities, the lake level is projected to rise even further\ncausing more widespread flooding, severe erosion, and substantial\nproperty damage. Conservative estimates say that the lake could rise\nanother 4 inches by mid-June but if more rain comes, the lake level\ncould rise another 9-12 inches.\n\nIn response to damage to local communities, Governor Cuomo declared a\nState of Emergency for the counties of Cayuga, Jefferson, Monroe,\nNiagara, Orleans, Oswego, Saint Lawrence and Wayne counties. As a\nresult, the National Guard has been deployed and the state expedited\nagency assistance to help communities impacted by record high lake\nlevels, flooding and coastal erosion.\n\nThis legislation seeks to provide financial assistance to property\nowners impacted by record high lake levels by providing assessment\nrelief. The Lake Ontario and Connected Waterways Assessment Relief Act\nmodels past state action in disaster relief, in particular, Superstorm\nSandy. Chapter 424 of 2013 provided local municipalities and school\ndistricts the authority to reduce assessments based on damages\nresultant of Sandy. In addition to Sandy, there is precedent in\nproviding assessment relief in other communities that have experienced\ndevastating floods (Part T, Chapter 44 2014 -Mohawk Valley and Niagara\nCounty Assessment Relief Act.)\n\nLike the previous disaster relief measures enacted in the state, the\nLake Ontario and Connected Waterways Assessment Relief authorizes\nlocalities, at local option, the ability to reduce assessments in\norder to reflect property damage or shoreline loss. Additionally, the\nact provides a funding mechanism to help local communities offset\nthese reductions in property assessments.\n\nTo help local municipalities provide the assessment reduction and\nbackstop the anticipated losses in property tax revenue, this proposal\ncreates a dedicated fund to provide support. Specifically, the\nlegislation creates a $100 million fund that local governments can\naccess on a first come, first serve basis, to offset the reductions in\nassessments. Revenue for this fund will derive from settlement money\nthat has yet to be appropriated. Currently, New York State has $287\nmillion in settlement money under this category.  As with previous\nassessment relief measures passed by the state, this bill seeks to\nprovide relief to property owners impacted by the flooding.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThe fund created by this bill would be capped at $100 million.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.,\n",
